-- EXPECT-ERROR: refinement violation
-- The composition proved by
-- `accept/t128_refine_list_contract_composes_call` has a limit: it fires only
-- when the caller's OWN contract already entails the callee's. Weakening
-- `outer`'s parameter to `{List(Int) | len(_) >= 0}` -- true of every list,
-- so it establishes nothing about `len(_) > 0` -- makes the plain `inner(ys)`
-- call SKIPPED, not proved: composition legitimately does not apply, and
-- exit 0 there would mean either "proved" or "silently ignored", exactly the
-- ambiguity an accept-only witness cannot resolve.
--
-- This file pins the case that matters: even through that same
-- uselessly-weak declared contract, the checker must still catch a REAL
-- failure once one exists. `List.length(ys) == 0` narrows `ys` to the empty
-- list via the ordinary runtime-guard alias (`t118`/`t117`'s mechanism),
-- independently of what `outer`'s own weak parameter refinement promises, so
-- the guarded `inner(ys)` is a DEFINITE failure and must be reported.
--
-- If this file ever starts exiting 0, either the weaker contract has begun
-- laundering the stronger requirement (composition applying where it must
-- not -- the false-positive risk `test_refinecheck.ml` calls "the false
-- positive that matters most"), or the `List.length` guard has stopped being
-- read inside a caller that itself carries a refined, but weaker, parameter.
mod T129 do

  fn inner(xs : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0}) : Int do
    match xs do
      Cons(h, _) -> h
      Nil        -> panic("empty")
    end
  end

  fn outer(ys : {List(Int) | len(_) >= 0}) : Int do
    if List.length(ys) == 0 do
      inner(ys)
    else
      0
    end
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    outer([1, 2, 3])
  end

end
